# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Database — Supabase is used ONLY as managed Postgres.
#
# There is no Supabase client, no Auth, no RLS, no anon key and no service-role
# key anywhere in this project. Access control is token-based and enforced in
# server actions. Both values below are Postgres connection strings and both are
# SERVER-SIDE SECRETS: never prefix them with NEXT_PUBLIC_.
#
# Find both in the Supabase dashboard under: Project Settings → Database →
# Connection string.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# POOLED — port 6543, Supavisor in *transaction* mode.
# This is what the running app uses. Vercel serverless spins up many short-lived
# instances and Supabase's direct connection limit is small, so every runtime
# query must go through the pooler.
#
# Transaction-mode pooling does not support prepared statements, which is why
# the postgres.js driver is configured with `prepare: false` (see src/db/client.ts).
# Omitting that produces intermittent failures under concurrency that look like
# application bugs.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.PROJECT_REF:YOUR_PASSWORD@aws-0-REGION.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres"

# DIRECT — port 5432, session mode.
# Used only by tooling that needs a session-scoped connection:
#   npm run db:migrate   (Drizzle DDL)
#   npm run db:export    (pg_dump backup)
# The app never opens this connection at runtime. Not needed in Vercel.
DIRECT_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.PROJECT_REF:YOUR_PASSWORD@aws-0-REGION.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres"
